# Panchayat Bill
Tamil title: ஊராட்சி மன்றச் சட்ட முன்வடிவு

The Honourable Ministers may, if they wish, argue, "We have granted powers to the administrative machinery so that it may function properly." But they cannot claim that adequate powers have been granted to the panchayat bodies composed of representatives elected by the people.

The Honourable Ministers, I, and members of parties other than my own in this House are representatives elected by the people. Panchayat members, likewise, are representatives elected by the people. Under the bill, however, there is a difference between the powers of members of the Legislative Assembly and those of panchayat members: panchayat members do not have adequate powers.

What will the rulers do if panchayat members placed in this position begin to ask, "The Honourable Ministers and the other members of the Legislative Assembly were elected by the people. We too were elected by the people. Members of the Legislative Assembly may have been elected by one hundred thousand people, while we were elected by five hundred. That is the only difference. Why should we not enjoy in the panchayat the same kind of rights that they enjoy? Why should we not possess such powers in the panchayat?"

The bill ought at least to provide for powers to be transferred to them gradually after they raise such questions. As the Honourable Finance Minister says, only those experienced in administration know and understand how the bill should provide for this; those without administrative experience neither know nor understand it, and they will have no opportunity to learn it either.

Members of many parties are participating in this debate precisely because amendments must be introduced into the bill in a manner that will remove the dissatisfaction village people may feel about defects in the law being enacted. It is therefore improper to ask whether further discussion in this House is necessary after the Select Committee has considered and decided upon the matter and shaped the bill in this form....

**C. Subramaniam:** Mr. Speaker, since the Honourable Member has referred to this several times, I wish to say one thing. I did not say that because the Select Committee had reached a decision, Honourable Members should neither examine the bill here nor speak about it. I would not say so.

I said only that if a provision in the bill is to be changed, there must be proper reasons for doing so and Honourable Members must point out those reasons. I merely asked that members should not go on demanding a change to a provision without giving proper reasons simply because one or two persons hold a different opinion about it.

I did not say that there was no room here to debate this bill, nor do I think so. Had it been decided that the House need not debate the bill, it would not have been decided to allow two days for general discussion and three days for clause-by-clause discussion.

**C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, I thank the Honourable Finance Minister for dispelling my fear. Since I do not belong to the ruling party, I feared that its members might decide that debates of this kind should no longer be held.

The opposition parties have the right to debate bills properly. I wish to state, with the utmost force and the utmost humility, that the reasons advanced by members of the ruling party for not granting administrative powers to village panchayats have neither satisfied our minds nor set them at rest.

Many have argued that village people at present lack the maturity and ability required to engage in administration. Those who have dealt directly with village people know well that our villages contain people of the calibre and ability needed to conduct bodies of local self-government of this kind.

Suppose that 7,000 panchayats can be established in Tamil Nadu. I humbly ask the Honourable Finance Minister to answer this question: does Tamil Nadu not have, for each of those 7,000 panchayats, people capable of carrying out works of public welfare efficiently and administering affairs with the utmost integrity? Does he have the courage to look at the village people and say, "You do not possess the administrative ability to run a body of local self-government efficiently. You have not yet attained the maturity required for it"...?

**T. L. Sasivarna Thevar:** Sir, on a point of order. The Leader of the Opposition is speaking in an important debate. At such a time, I wish to know whether it is proper for the Finance Minister to sit in the House looking through Government files.

**Mr. Speaker:** He may look at files related to the debate.

**C. N. Annadurai:** Does our Finance Minister have the courage to look at the village people and say, "You lack administrative ability, and therefore we are not going to entrust administrative responsibility to you"?

How many of those who have administered panchayats thus far have been arrested for misconduct? Against how many presidents have disciplinary proceedings been taken? How many presidents have committed misappropriation? If we could compare those figures with the number of administrative officers who have behaved badly, and examine which of the two groups has behaved worse, we would certainly understand that entrusting panchayat responsibilities to the presidents would not impair the administration. That is what I wish to place before this Legislative Assembly.
